WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.300
Streaming and chunked processing let a constrained C program handle data without storing the entire input in memory.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:15.800
This matters for files, network messages, serial protocols, logs, update packages, sensor streams, and encoded records.

3
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.300
The safer design processes bounded pieces and keeps parser state small.

4
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.260
A streaming parser should enforce maximums.

5
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:35.560
Even if the program reads data in chunks, it still needs a maximum frame size, maximum field length, maximum nesting level where relevant, and maximum amount of retained state.

6
00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:40.220
Without those limits, incremental code can still accumulate too much data.

7
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.700
Length validation must happen before storage.

8
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:53.560
A length field should be checked against protocol limits, destination capacity, remaining input, and product policy before a buffer is filled or an object is allocated.

9
00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:58.140
The parser should not trust a length simply because it was read from a familiar format.

10
00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.260
Partial input is normal.

11
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.920
A chunk may end in the middle of a header, string, record, or checksum.

12
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:11.020
The parser should have a clear state machine that records only the minimal state needed to continue.

13
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.560
State transitions should be explicit enough that reviewers can see how malformed input is rejected.

14
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:23.240
Ring buffers and other bounded buffers can be useful when they have clear ownership and capacity rules.

15
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:31.260
The code should define what happens when the buffer fills, whether data may be dropped, how consumers catch up, and how message boundaries are preserved.

16
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.420
Safe rejection is part of parser design.

17
00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:45.000
Oversized or malformed input should lead to a predictable error path that resets parser state, releases owned resources, and keeps later input from being interpreted in the wrong context.

18
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:50.140
The parser should not leave half-built objects where later code treats them as valid.

19
00:01:50.420 --> 00:02:00.020
Tests should include boundary sizes, partial chunks, missing terminators, invalid lengths, repeated small fragments, and full-buffer conditions.

20
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:07.320
The purpose is to prove that the parser remains bounded and understandable even when input arrives in inconvenient shapes.
